hermes v0.23.0: GET /api/sessions/{id}/messages revalidates (ETag, 304).

Upstream: the history page has no validator. Clients re-read an open
chat's tail on every sessions.changed tick, which any write to the
profile's state.db fires (other chats, cron, subagents), and get the
same bytes again: Hermes Desktop pulled ~380 KB every ~10 s for a long
chat that hadn't changed (124 MB of 189 MB in 5.5 h on Dave's laptop).

This patch: the page carries `ETag: W/"<first 32 hex of the sha256 of
the JSON body>"` and `Cache-Control: private, no-cache`; a request whose
If-None-Match holds that tag (weak comparison, `*`, lists) gets a 304
without a body. The tag is the body's own hash, so every change the
page would show (appends, tool rows, in-place edits, rewinds,
compaction, a rewritten notice) changes it and nothing else does: no
stored marker to miss a write. Weak, because nginx's gzip re-encodes
the body and weakens a strong tag. The server still reads and projects
the page (the cost is the database read, not the transfer). Direct
Python callers (no Request) get the dict as before.

Tests: tests/hermes_cli/test_session_messages_etag.py.

